#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186702956 :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=\\left(3 x + 3\\right)^{4}$, find $f^{\\prime}(x).$\n\nUse the decomposition $g(x)=3 x + 3$ and $F(u)=u^{4}$, so $f(x)=F(g(x))$. Recall that $f^{\\prime}(x)=F^{\\prime}(g(x))g^{\\prime}(x)$." :question/worked-solution "Let $g(x)=3 x + 3$ and $F(g)=g^{4}$, so $f(x)=F(g(x))$.\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} F^{\\prime}(g)&=4 g^{3}, \\\\ g^{\\prime}(x)&=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the chain rule,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} f^{\\prime}(x)&=F^{\\prime}(g(x))\\cdot g^{\\prime}(x) \\\\ &=\\left(4 \\left(3 x + 3\\right)^{3}\\right)\\cdot\\left(3\\right) \\\\ &=12 \\left(3 x + 3\\right)^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186702968 :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=\\left(- 2 x - 2\\right)^{3}$, find $f^{\\prime}(x).$\n\nUse the decomposition $g(x)=- 2 x - 2$ and $F(u)=u^{3}$, so $f(x)=F(g(x))$. Recall that $f^{\\prime}(x)=F^{\\prime}(g(x))g^{\\prime}(x)$." :question/worked-solution "Let $g(x)=- 2 x - 2$ and $F(g)=g^{3}$, so $f(x)=F(g(x))$.\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} F^{\\prime}(g)&=3 g^{2}, \\\\ g^{\\prime}(x)&=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the chain rule,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} f^{\\prime}(x)&=F^{\\prime}(g(x))\\cdot g^{\\prime}(x) \\\\ &=\\left(3 \\left(- 2 x - 2\\right)^{2}\\right)\\cdot\\left(-2\\right) \\\\ &=- 6 \\left(- 2 x - 2\\right)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186702969 :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=\\left(2 x - 2\\right)^{3}$, find $f^{\\prime}(x).$\n\nUse the decomposition $g(x)=2 x - 2$ and $F(u)=u^{3}$, so $f(x)=F(g(x))$. Recall that $f^{\\prime}(x)=F^{\\prime}(g(x))g^{\\prime}(x)$." :question/worked-solution "Let $g(x)=2 x - 2$ and $F(g)=g^{3}$, so $f(x)=F(g(x))$.\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} F^{\\prime}(g)&=3 g^{2}, \\\\ g^{\\prime}(x)&=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the chain rule,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} f^{\\prime}(x)&=F^{\\prime}(g(x))\\cdot g^{\\prime}(x) \\\\ &=\\left(3 \\left(2 x - 2\\right)^{2}\\right)\\cdot\\left(2\\right) \\\\ &=6 \\left(2 x - 2\\right)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186702970 :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=\\left(2 - 3 x\\right)^{3}$, find $f^{\\prime}(x).$\n\nUse the decomposition $g(x)=2 - 3 x$ and $F(u)=u^{3}$, so $f(x)=F(g(x))$. Recall that $f^{\\prime}(x)=F^{\\prime}(g(x))g^{\\prime}(x)$." :question/worked-solution "Let $g(x)=2 - 3 x$ and $F(g)=g^{3}$, so $f(x)=F(g(x))$.\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} F^{\\prime}(g)&=3 g^{2}, \\\\ g^{\\prime}(x)&=-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the chain rule,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} f^{\\prime}(x)&=F^{\\prime}(g(x))\\cdot g^{\\prime}(x) \\\\ &=\\left(3 \\left(2 - 3 x\\right)^{2}\\right)\\cdot\\left(-3\\right) \\\\ &=- 9 \\left(2 - 3 x\\right)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186702980 :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf a=\\langle 2,0,0\\rangle$ and $\\mathbf b=\\langle 0,2,x\\rangle$. Given that $|\\mathbf a\\times\\mathbf b|=2 \\sqrt{5}$, find all real values of $x$.\n\nUse the determinant setup\n\n$$\n\\mathbf a\\times\\mathbf b=\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf i&\\mathbf j&\\mathbf k\\\\2&0&0\\\\0&2&x\\end{vmatrix}.\n$$\n\nRecall that the magnitude of $\\langle u,v,w\\rangle$ is $\\sqrt{u^2+v^2+w^2}$.\n\nIn **ascending order**, the values are $x=$ {{field-1}} and $x=$ {{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "Using the determinant formula, compute each signed component:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf a\\times\\mathbf b&=\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf i&\\mathbf j&\\mathbf k\\\\2&0&0\\\\0&2&x\\end{vmatrix}\\\\\nc_1&=\\begin{vmatrix}0&0\\\\2&x\\end{vmatrix}\\\\\n&=((0)(x)-(0)(2))=0\\\\\nc_2&=-\\begin{vmatrix}2&0\\\\0&x\\end{vmatrix}\\\\\n&=-((2)(x)-(0)(0))=- 2x\\\\\nc_3&=\\begin{vmatrix}2&0\\\\0&2\\end{vmatrix}\\\\\n&=((2)(2)-(0)(0))=4\\\\\n\\mathbf a\\times\\mathbf b&=\\langle 0,- 2x,4\\rangle\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $N(x)$ be the sum of the squared components. Compute the magnitude, square both sides, and solve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nN(x)&=(0)^2\\\\\n&\\quad +(- 2x)^2\\\\\n&\\quad +(4)^2\\\\\n&=4x^{2} + 16\\\\\n|\\mathbf a\\times\\mathbf b|&=\\sqrt{4x^{2} + 16}\\\\\n\\sqrt{4x^{2} + 16}&=2 \\sqrt{5}\\\\\n4x^{2} + 16&=20\\\\\n4x^2&=4\\\\\nx^2&=1\\\\\nx&=\\pm\\sqrt{1}\\\\\nx&=-1\\quad\\text{or}\\quad x=1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCheck each candidate in the squared magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nN(-1)&=20=20\\\\\nN(1)&=20=20\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere $N(x)$ is the sum of the squared cross-product components. Both magnitudes are nonnegative, so these checks also verify the original magnitude equation. In ascending order, all real solutions are $x=-1$ and $x=1$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186702985 :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf a=\\langle 1,0,0\\rangle$ and $\\mathbf b=\\langle 0,x,-1\\rangle$. Given that $|\\mathbf a\\times\\mathbf b|=\\sqrt{2}$, find all real values of $x$.\n\nUse the determinant setup\n\n$$\n\\mathbf a\\times\\mathbf b=\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf i&\\mathbf j&\\mathbf k\\\\1&0&0\\\\0&x&-1\\end{vmatrix}.\n$$\n\nRecall that the magnitude of $\\langle u,v,w\\rangle$ is $\\sqrt{u^2+v^2+w^2}$.\n\nIn **ascending order**, the values are $x=$ {{field-1}} and $x=$ {{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "Using the determinant formula, compute each signed component:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf a\\times\\mathbf b&=\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf i&\\mathbf j&\\mathbf k\\\\1&0&0\\\\0&x&-1\\end{vmatrix}\\\\\nc_1&=\\begin{vmatrix}0&0\\\\x&-1\\end{vmatrix}\\\\\n&=((0)(-1)-(0)(x))=0\\\\\nc_2&=-\\begin{vmatrix}1&0\\\\0&-1\\end{vmatrix}\\\\\n&=-((1)(-1)-(0)(0))=1\\\\\nc_3&=\\begin{vmatrix}1&0\\\\0&x\\end{vmatrix}\\\\\n&=((1)(x)-(0)(0))=x\\\\\n\\mathbf a\\times\\mathbf b&=\\langle 0,1,x\\rangle\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $N(x)$ be the sum of the squared components. Compute the magnitude, square both sides, and solve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nN(x)&=(0)^2\\\\\n&\\quad +(1)^2\\\\\n&\\quad +(x)^2\\\\\n&=x^{2} + 1\\\\\n|\\mathbf a\\times\\mathbf b|&=\\sqrt{x^{2} + 1}\\\\\n\\sqrt{x^{2} + 1}&=\\sqrt{2}\\\\\nx^{2} + 1&=2\\\\\nx^2&=1\\\\\nx^2&=1\\\\\nx&=\\pm\\sqrt{1}\\\\\nx&=-1\\quad\\text{or}\\quad x=1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCheck each candidate in the squared magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nN(-1)&=2=2\\\\\nN(1)&=2=2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere $N(x)$ is the sum of the squared cross-product components. Both magnitudes are nonnegative, so these checks also verify the original magnitude equation. In ascending order, all real solutions are $x=-1$ and $x=1$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186702988 :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf a=\\langle x,-1,0\\rangle$ and $\\mathbf b=\\langle 0,0,2\\rangle$. Given that $|\\mathbf a\\times\\mathbf b|=2 \\sqrt{2}$, find all real values of $x$.\n\nUse the determinant setup\n\n$$\n\\mathbf a\\times\\mathbf b=\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf i&\\mathbf j&\\mathbf k\\\\x&-1&0\\\\0&0&2\\end{vmatrix}.\n$$\n\nRecall that the magnitude of $\\langle u,v,w\\rangle$ is $\\sqrt{u^2+v^2+w^2}$.\n\nIn **ascending order**, the values are $x=$ {{field-1}} and $x=$ {{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "Using the determinant formula, compute each signed component:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf a\\times\\mathbf b&=\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf i&\\mathbf j&\\mathbf k\\\\x&-1&0\\\\0&0&2\\end{vmatrix}\\\\\nc_1&=\\begin{vmatrix}-1&0\\\\0&2\\end{vmatrix}\\\\\n&=((-1)(2)-(0)(0))=-2\\\\\nc_2&=-\\begin{vmatrix}x&0\\\\0&2\\end{vmatrix}\\\\\n&=-((x)(2)-(0)(0))=- 2x\\\\\nc_3&=\\begin{vmatrix}x&-1\\\\0&0\\end{vmatrix}\\\\\n&=((x)(0)-(-1)(0))=0\\\\\n\\mathbf a\\times\\mathbf b&=\\langle -2,- 2x,0\\rangle\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $N(x)$ be the sum of the squared components. Compute the magnitude, square both sides, and solve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nN(x)&=(-2)^2\\\\\n&\\quad +(- 2x)^2\\\\\n&\\quad +(0)^2\\\\\n&=4x^{2} + 4\\\\\n|\\mathbf a\\times\\mathbf b|&=\\sqrt{4x^{2} + 4}\\\\\n\\sqrt{4x^{2} + 4}&=2 \\sqrt{2}\\\\\n4x^{2} + 4&=8\\\\\n4x^2&=4\\\\\nx^2&=1\\\\\nx&=\\pm\\sqrt{1}\\\\\nx&=-1\\quad\\text{or}\\quad x=1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCheck each candidate in the squared magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nN(-1)&=8=8\\\\\nN(1)&=8=8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere $N(x)$ is the sum of the squared cross-product components. Both magnitudes are nonnegative, so these checks also verify the original magnitude equation. In ascending order, all real solutions are $x=-1$ and $x=1$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186702992 :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf a=\\langle x,0,1\\rangle$ and $\\mathbf b=\\langle 0,2,0\\rangle$. Given that $|\\mathbf a\\times\\mathbf b|=2 \\sqrt{17}$, find all real values of $x$.\n\nUse the determinant setup\n\n$$\n\\mathbf a\\times\\mathbf b=\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf i&\\mathbf j&\\mathbf k\\\\x&0&1\\\\0&2&0\\end{vmatrix}.\n$$\n\nRecall that the magnitude of $\\langle u,v,w\\rangle$ is $\\sqrt{u^2+v^2+w^2}$.\n\nIn **ascending order**, the values are $x=$ {{field-1}} and $x=$ {{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "Using the determinant formula, compute each signed component:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf a\\times\\mathbf b&=\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf i&\\mathbf j&\\mathbf k\\\\x&0&1\\\\0&2&0\\end{vmatrix}\\\\\nc_1&=\\begin{vmatrix}0&1\\\\2&0\\end{vmatrix}\\\\\n&=((0)(0)-(1)(2))=-2\\\\\nc_2&=-\\begin{vmatrix}x&1\\\\0&0\\end{vmatrix}\\\\\n&=-((x)(0)-(1)(0))=0\\\\\nc_3&=\\begin{vmatrix}x&0\\\\0&2\\end{vmatrix}\\\\\n&=((x)(2)-(0)(0))=2x\\\\\n\\mathbf a\\times\\mathbf b&=\\langle -2,0,2x\\rangle\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $N(x)$ be the sum of the squared components. Compute the magnitude, square both sides, and solve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nN(x)&=(-2)^2\\\\\n&\\quad +(0)^2\\\\\n&\\quad +(2x)^2\\\\\n&=4x^{2} + 4\\\\\n|\\mathbf a\\times\\mathbf b|&=\\sqrt{4x^{2} + 4}\\\\\n\\sqrt{4x^{2} + 4}&=2 \\sqrt{17}\\\\\n4x^{2} + 4&=68\\\\\n4x^2&=64\\\\\nx^2&=16\\\\\nx&=\\pm\\sqrt{16}\\\\\nx&=-4\\quad\\text{or}\\quad x=4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCheck each candidate in the squared magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nN(-4)&=68=68\\\\\nN(4)&=68=68\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere $N(x)$ is the sum of the squared cross-product components. Both magnitudes are nonnegative, so these checks also verify the original magnitude equation. In ascending order, all real solutions are $x=-4$ and $x=4$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186703009 :question/problem "Which graph shows the line $y=-x-1$?\n\nFirst give the two points on this line with $x=-2$ and $x=1$. Enter each answer as an ordered pair $(x,y)$, then select the graph.\n\nPoint with $x=-2$: {{point-first}}\n\nPoint with $x=1$: {{point-second}}" :question/worked-solution "We need to find two distinct points that lie on our line. Any two points will do!\n\n- For the first point, we will let the $x$-coordinate be $x=-2.$ Then the $y$-coordinate is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-x-1 \\\\  & =-(-2)-1 \\\\  & =2-1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the first point is $(-2,1).$\n\n- For the second point, we will let the $x$-coordinate be $x=1.$ Then the $y$-coordinate is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-x-1 \\\\  & =-(1)-1 \\\\  & =-1-1 \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second point is $(1,-2).$\n\nFinally, we plot those two points on the $xy$-plane and draw a line through them. This is the graph of $y=-x-1.$\n\n![](images/28/2872e56343d018e4b0b1abb42632e3ebfb7b623108535564afe4af1ca86d976b.png)\n\nAll choices have the same $y$-intercept. Check another point to distinguish the lines."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186703010 :question/problem "Which graph shows the line $y=1-x$?\n\nFirst give the two points on this line with $x=-2$ and $x=2$. Enter each answer as an ordered pair $(x,y)$, then select the graph.\n\nPoint with $x=-2$: {{point-first}}\n\nPoint with $x=2$: {{point-second}}" :question/worked-solution "We need to find two distinct points that lie on our line. Any two points will do!\n\n- For the first point, we will let the $x$-coordinate be $x=-2.$ Then the $y$-coordinate is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =1-x \\\\  & =1-(-2) \\\\  & =1+2 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the first point is $(-2,3).$\n\n- For the second point, we will let the $x$-coordinate be $x=2.$ Then the $y$-coordinate is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =1-x \\\\  & =1-(2) \\\\  & =1-2 \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second point is $(2,-1).$\n\nFinally, we plot those two points on the $xy$-plane and draw a line through them. This is the graph of $y=1-x.$\n\n![](images/d2/d2f018d147362771ada1a59c98214c4df5bcac60129243ec6a7bb206794b13c9.png)\n\nAll choices have the same $y$-intercept. Check another point to distinguish the lines."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186703032 :question/problem "Which graph shows the line $y=-1+\\frac{1}{3}x$?\n\nFirst give the two points on this line with $x=-3$ and $x=3$. Enter each answer as an ordered pair $(x,y)$, then select the graph.\n\nPoint with $x=-3$: {{point-first}}\n\nPoint with $x=3$: {{point-second}}" :question/worked-solution "We need to find two distinct points that lie on our line. Any two points will do!\n\n- For the first point, we will let the $x$-coordinate be $x=-3.$ Then the $y$-coordinate is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-1+\\frac{1}{3}x \\\\  & =-1+\\frac{1}{3}(-3) \\\\  & =-1-1 \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the first point is $(-3,-2).$\n\n- For the second point, we will let the $x$-coordinate be $x=3.$ Then the $y$-coordinate is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-1+\\frac{1}{3}x \\\\  & =-1+\\frac{1}{3}(3) \\\\  & =-1+1 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second point is $(3,0).$\n\nFinally, we plot those two points on the $xy$-plane and draw a line through them. This is the graph of $y=-1+\\frac{1}{3}x.$\n\n![](images/6b/6bb16e235d5a29fb9c99c91a7af7ef7af5cdec31ed7d091167d30d993b5ff511.png)\n\nAll choices have the same $y$-intercept. Check another point to distinguish the lines."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186703045 :question/problem "Which graph shows the line $y=\\frac{1}{2}x-1$?\n\nFirst give the two points on this line with $x=-4$ and $x=4$. Enter each answer as an ordered pair $(x,y)$, then select the graph.\n\nPoint with $x=-4$: {{point-first}}\n\nPoint with $x=4$: {{point-second}}" :question/worked-solution "We need to find two distinct points that lie on our line. Any two points will do!\n\n- For the first point, we will let the $x$-coordinate be $x=-4.$ Then the $y$-coordinate is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{2}x-1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(-4)-1 \\\\  & =-2-1 \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the first point is $(-4,-3).$\n\n- For the second point, we will let the $x$-coordinate be $x=4.$ Then the $y$-coordinate is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{2}x-1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(4)-1 \\\\  & =2-1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second point is $(4,1).$\n\nFinally, we plot those two points on the $xy$-plane and draw a line through them. This is the graph of $y=\\frac{1}{2}x-1.$\n\n![](images/ce/ce0997e42327f0ced575bb28ed6f5428efea8cd94eeef100bb98c31c3c33f4a5.png)\n\nAll choices have the same $y$-intercept. Check another point to distinguish the lines."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186703047 :question/problem "Which graph shows the line $y=-1+2x$?\n\nFirst give the two points on this line with $x=-1$ and $x=2$. Enter each answer as an ordered pair $(x,y)$, then select the graph.\n\nPoint with $x=-1$: {{point-first}}\n\nPoint with $x=2$: {{point-second}}" :question/worked-solution "We need to find two distinct points that lie on our line. Any two points will do!\n\n- For the first point, we will let the $x$-coordinate be $x=-1.$ Then the $y$-coordinate is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-1+2x \\\\  & =-1+2(-1) \\\\  & =-1-2 \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the first point is $(-1,-3).$\n\n- For the second point, we will let the $x$-coordinate be $x=2.$ Then the $y$-coordinate is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-1+2x \\\\  & =-1+2(2) \\\\  & =-1+4 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second point is $(2,3).$\n\nFinally, we plot those two points on the $xy$-plane and draw a line through them. This is the graph of $y=-1+2x.$\n\n![](images/c7/c7162c5055ef64b0b61c03df4eedf11c705ecd7bb5ae4b83abe850b03c4484cd.png)\n\nAll choices have the same $y$-intercept. Check another point to distinguish the lines."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186703068 :question/problem "Use two points to sketch $y=2-2x$.\n\nComplete the two $y$-coordinates, then choose the graph through both points.\n\nWhen $x=0$, $y=$ {{y-first}}.\n\nWhen $x=-1$, $y=$ {{y-second}}." :question/worked-solution "We need to find two distinct points that lie on our line. Any two points will do!\n\n- For the first point, we will let the $x$-coordinate be $x=0.$ Then the $y$-coordinate is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2-2x \\\\  & =2-2(0) \\\\  & =2-0 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the first point is $(0,2).$\n\n- For the second point, we will let the $x$-coordinate be $x=-1.$ Then the $y$-coordinate is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2-2x \\\\  & =2-2(-1) \\\\  & =2+2 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second point is $(-1,4).$\n\nFinally, we plot those two points on the $xy$-plane and draw a line through them. This is the graph of $y=2-2x.$\n\n![](images/74/740fabee46d03c5690c52e82274b31a48abffd4e3d7c576dd207cddce2180c36.png)\n\nAll choices have the same $y$-intercept. Check another point to distinguish the lines."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186703075 :question/problem "Use two points to sketch $y=\\frac{1}{3}x$.\n\nComplete the two $y$-coordinates, then choose the graph through both points.\n\nWhen $x=0$, $y=$ {{y-first}}.\n\nWhen $x=3$, $y=$ {{y-second}}." :question/worked-solution "We need to find two distinct points that lie on our line. Any two points will do!\n\n- For the first point, we will let the $x$-coordinate be $x=0.$ Then the $y$-coordinate is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{3}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(0) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the first point is $(0,0).$\n\n- For the second point, we will let the $x$-coordinate be $x=3.$ Then the $y$-coordinate is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{3}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(3) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second point is $(3,1).$\n\nFinally, we plot those two points on the $xy$-plane and draw a line through them. This is the graph of $y=\\frac{1}{3}x.$ \n\n![](images/82/827fd626bc6c0286fba1f6a61b52c1a30c2ed6cf0f13c2940888a30af87472cf.png)\n\nAll choices have the same $y$-intercept. Check another point to distinguish the lines."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186703078 :question/problem "Use two points to sketch $y=-x+2$.\n\nComplete the two $y$-coordinates, then choose the graph through both points.\n\nWhen $x=0$, $y=$ {{y-first}}.\n\nWhen $x=1$, $y=$ {{y-second}}." :question/worked-solution "We need to find two distinct points that lie on our line. Any two points will do!\n\n- For the first point, we will let the $x$-coordinate be $x=0.$ Then the $y$-coordinate is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-x+2 \\\\  & =-(0)+2 \\\\  & =0+2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the first point is $(0,2).$\n\n- For the second point, we will let the $x$-coordinate be $x=1.$ Then the $y$-coordinate is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-x+2 \\\\  & =-(1)+2 \\\\  & =-1+2 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second point is $(1,1).$\n\nFinally, we plot those two points on the $xy$-plane and draw a line through them. This is the graph of $y=-x+2.$\n\n![](images/a7/a7adc391484a7a183400fff460abfb7ae39494ba19e934303474c1165633b60e.png)\n\nAll choices have the same $y$-intercept. Check another point to distinguish the lines."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186703097 :question/problem "Use two points to sketch $y=1+\\frac{1}{2}x$.\n\nComplete the two $y$-coordinates, then choose the graph through both points.\n\nWhen $x=0$, $y=$ {{y-first}}.\n\nWhen $x=-4$, $y=$ {{y-second}}." :question/worked-solution "We need to find two distinct points that lie on our line. Any two points will do!\n\n- For the first point, we will let the $x$-coordinate be $x=0.$ Then the $y$-coordinate is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =1+\\frac{1}{2}x \\\\  & =1+\\frac{1}{2}(0) \\\\  & =1+0 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the first point is $(0,1).$\n\n- For the second point, we will let the $x$-coordinate be $x=-4.$ Then the $y$-coordinate is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =1+\\frac{1}{2}x \\\\  & =1+\\frac{1}{2}(-4) \\\\  & =1-2 \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second point is $(-4,-1).$\n\nFinally, we plot those two points on the $xy$-plane and draw a line through them. This is the graph of $y=1+\\frac{1}{2}x.$\n\n![](images/ec/ec1e2afb0ca460fabf39276605226cbc17078fe92ff79704d4ddf1ce08a9f593.png)\n\nAll choices have the same $y$-intercept. Check another point to distinguish the lines."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186703109 :question/problem "Which graph shows the line $y=-2x+1$?\n\nFirst give the two points on this line with $x=-2$ and $x=1$. Enter each answer as an ordered pair $(x,y)$, then select the graph.\n\nPoint with $x=-2$: {{point-first}}\n\nPoint with $x=1$: {{point-second}}" :question/worked-solution "We need to find two distinct points that lie on our line. Any two points will do!\n\n- For the first point, we will let the $x$-coordinate be $x=-2.$ Then the $y$-coordinate is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-2x+1 \\\\  & =-2(-2)+1 \\\\  & =4+1 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the first point is $(-2,5).$\n\n- For the second point, we will let the $x$-coordinate be $x=1.$ Then the $y$-coordinate is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-2x+1 \\\\  & =-2(1)+1 \\\\  & =-2+1 \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second point is $(1,-1).$\n\nFinally, we plot those two points on the $xy$-plane and draw a line through them. This is the graph of $y=-2x+1.$\n\n![](images/ff/ff833dec09278cf2f9ad1d6642246e53f67a3a2a0352e6c016e6a7c888f0da16.png)\n\nAll choices have the same $y$-intercept. Check another point to distinguish the lines."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186703125 :question/problem "Which graph shows the line $y=\\frac{1}{3}x$?\n\nFirst give the two points on this line with $x=-3$ and $x=6$. Enter each answer as an ordered pair $(x,y)$, then select the graph.\n\nPoint with $x=-3$: {{point-first}}\n\nPoint with $x=6$: {{point-second}}" :question/worked-solution "We need to find two distinct points that lie on our line. Any two points will do!\n\n- For the first point, we will let the $x$-coordinate be $x=-3.$ Then the $y$-coordinate is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{3}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(-3) \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the first point is $(-3,-1).$\n\n- For the second point, we will let the $x$-coordinate be $x=6.$ Then the $y$-coordinate is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{3}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(6) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second point is $(6,2).$\n\nFinally, we plot those two points on the $xy$-plane and draw a line through them. This is the graph of $y=\\frac{1}{3}x.$ \n\n![](images/a7/a7ee4360ef9e4466005d2ff3652357f804c057a4645653eaafde9adf2dc5886f.png)\n\nAll choices have the same $y$-intercept. Check another point to distinguish the lines."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186703128 :question/problem "Which graph shows the line $y=1-\\frac{1}{2}x$?\n\nFirst give the two points on this line with $x=-4$ and $x=2$. Enter each answer as an ordered pair $(x,y)$, then select the graph.\n\nPoint with $x=-4$: {{point-first}}\n\nPoint with $x=2$: {{point-second}}" :question/worked-solution "We need to find two distinct points that lie on our line. Any two points will do!\n\n- For the first point, we will let the $x$-coordinate be $x=-4.$ Then the $y$-coordinate is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =1-\\frac{1}{2}x \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{2}(-4) \\\\  & =1+2 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the first point is $(-4,3).$\n\n- For the second point, we will let the $x$-coordinate be $x=2.$ Then the $y$-coordinate is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =1-\\frac{1}{2}x \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{2}(2) \\\\  & =1-1 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second point is $(2,0).$\n\nFinally, we plot those two points on the $xy$-plane and draw a line through them. This is the graph of $y=1-\\frac{1}{2}x.$\n\n![](images/2d/2d4a9de0be8d3619a976daa775f9cbd56ecbd1a43a400ac573d67464db3e62eb.png)\n\nAll choices have the same $y$-intercept. Check another point to distinguish the lines."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186703141 :question/problem "Which graph shows the line $y=2x-1$?\n\nFirst give the two points on this line with $x=-1$ and $x=1$. Enter each answer as an ordered pair $(x,y)$, then select the graph.\n\nPoint with $x=-1$: {{point-first}}\n\nPoint with $x=1$: {{point-second}}" :question/worked-solution "We need to find two distinct points that lie on our line. Any two points will do!\n\n- For the first point, we will let the $x$-coordinate be $x=-1.$ Then the $y$-coordinate is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2x-1 \\\\  & =2(-1)-1 \\\\  & =-2-1 \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the first point is $(-1,-3).$\n\n- For the second point, we will let the $x$-coordinate be $x=1.$ Then the $y$-coordinate is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2x-1 \\\\  & =2(1)-1 \\\\  & =2-1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second point is $(1,1).$\n\nFinally, we plot those two points on the $xy$-plane and draw a line through them. This is the graph of $y=2x-1.$\n\n![](images/4c/4cb2d4bfae77b9d980d11ccb56c8ef32e3e0abb6418d1aee8e4053c0cc2021c7.png)\n\nAll choices have the same $y$-intercept. Check another point to distinguish the lines."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186703155 :question/problem "Which graph shows the line $y=-x$?\n\nFirst give the two points on this line with $x=-2$ and $x=1$. Enter each answer as an ordered pair $(x,y)$, then select the graph.\n\nPoint with $x=-2$: {{point-first}}\n\nPoint with $x=1$: {{point-second}}" :question/worked-solution "We need to find two distinct points that lie on our line. Any two points will do!\n\n- For the first point, we will let the $x$-coordinate be $x=-2.$ Then the $y$-coordinate is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-x \\\\  & =-(-2) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the first point is $(-2,2).$\n\n- For the second point, we will let the $x$-coordinate be $x=1.$ Then the $y$-coordinate is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-x \\\\  & =-(1) \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second point is $(1,-1).$\n\nFinally, we plot those two points on the $xy$-plane and draw a line through them. This is the graph of $y=-x.$ \n\n![](images/32/32ebf89997d1dd0f10e20cc086c36ca5462819b3625a00d15e8ae98a023a4651.png)\n\nAll choices have the same $y$-intercept. Check another point to distinguish the lines."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186703391 :question/problem "Find $\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}$ for $f(x,y,z)=- x^{3} y + z^{3}$. Treat $x, z$ as constants." :question/worked-solution "Differentiate with respect to $y$, holding $x$ and $z$ fixed. The term involving only $z$ differentiates to zero. Use the constant multiple and power rules.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}&=- x^{3}\\cdot 1+0\\\\&=- x^{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186703397 :question/problem "Find $\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}$ for $f(x,y,z)=2 x^{3} + 2 y z^{2}$. Treat $x, z$ as constants." :question/worked-solution "Differentiate with respect to $y$, holding $x$ and $z$ fixed. The term involving only $x$ differentiates to zero. Use the constant multiple and power rules.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}&=2 z^{2}\\cdot 1+0\\\\&=2 z^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186703398 :question/problem "Find $\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}$ for $f(x,y,z)=3 x y^{3} + 2 z^{2}$. Treat $x, z$ as constants." :question/worked-solution "Differentiate with respect to $y$, holding $x$ and $z$ fixed. The term involving only $z$ differentiates to zero. Use the constant multiple and power rules.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}&=3 x\\cdot 3 y^{2}+0\\\\&=9 x y^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186703403 :question/problem "Find $\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial z}$ for $f(x,y,z)=x z^{3} + 2 y^{3}$. Treat $x, y$ as constants." :question/worked-solution "Differentiate with respect to $z$, holding $x$ and $y$ fixed. The term involving only $y$ differentiates to zero. Use the constant multiple and power rules.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial z}&=x\\cdot 3 z^{2}+0\\\\&=3 x z^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186703527 :question/problem "For the function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{e^{- x y^{2}}}{x - y^{2} + 2},\n$$\n\nfind $\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}$, where $x - y^{2} + 2\\ne0$." :question/worked-solution "Hold $x$ constant and differentiate with respect to $y$. Write $N=e^{- x y^{2}}$ and $D=x - y^{2} + 2$. The component derivatives are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nN_{y}&=- 2 x y e^{- x y^{2}}, \\\\\nD_{y}&=- 2 y.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe chain rule differentiates the exponent first; its inner derivative is $- 2 x y$. Apply the quotient rule, keeping its subtraction and squared denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}&=\\frac{N_{y}D-ND_{y}}{D^2} \\\\\n&=\\frac{\\left(- 2 x y e^{- x y^{2}}\\right)D-N\\left(- 2 y\\right)}{D^2} \\\\\n&=\\frac{e^{- x y^{2}}\\left(- 2 x^{2} y + 2 x y^{3} - 4 x y + 2 y\\right)}{\\left(x - y^{2} + 2\\right)^2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last step factors the common nonzero exponential $e^{- x y^{2}}$ from the numerator.  These expressions apply where $x - y^{2} + 2\\ne0$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186703534 :question/problem "For the function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{e^{x y^{2}}}{x + y^{2} - 2},\n$$\n\nfind $\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}$, where $x + y^{2} - 2\\ne0$." :question/worked-solution "Hold $y$ constant and differentiate with respect to $x$. Write $N=e^{x y^{2}}$ and $D=x + y^{2} - 2$. The component derivatives are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nN_{x}&=y^{2} e^{x y^{2}}, \\\\\nD_{x}&=1.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe chain rule differentiates the exponent first; its inner derivative is $y^{2}$. Apply the quotient rule, keeping its subtraction and squared denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}&=\\frac{N_{x}D-ND_{x}}{D^2} \\\\\n&=\\frac{\\left(y^{2} e^{x y^{2}}\\right)D-N\\left(1\\right)}{D^2} \\\\\n&=\\frac{e^{x y^{2}}\\left(x y^{2} + y^{4} - 2 y^{2} - 1\\right)}{\\left(x + y^{2} - 2\\right)^2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last step factors the common nonzero exponential $e^{x y^{2}}$ from the numerator.  These expressions apply where $x + y^{2} - 2\\ne0$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186703542 :question/problem "For the function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{e^{x y^{2}}}{x + y^{2}},\n$$\n\nfind $\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}$, where $x + y^{2}\\ne0$." :question/worked-solution "Hold $y$ constant and differentiate with respect to $x$. Write $N=e^{x y^{2}}$ and $D=x + y^{2}$. The component derivatives are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nN_{x}&=y^{2} e^{x y^{2}}, \\\\\nD_{x}&=1.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe chain rule differentiates the exponent first; its inner derivative is $y^{2}$. Apply the quotient rule, keeping its subtraction and squared denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}&=\\frac{N_{x}D-ND_{x}}{D^2} \\\\\n&=\\frac{\\left(y^{2} e^{x y^{2}}\\right)D-N\\left(1\\right)}{D^2} \\\\\n&=\\frac{e^{x y^{2}}\\left(x y^{2} + y^{4} - 1\\right)}{\\left(x + y^{2}\\right)^2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last step factors the common nonzero exponential $e^{x y^{2}}$ from the numerator.  These expressions apply where $x + y^{2}\\ne0$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186703543 :question/problem "For the function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{e^{- x y^{2}}}{x - y^{2} + 1},\n$$\n\nfind $\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}$, where $x - y^{2} + 1\\ne0$." :question/worked-solution "Hold $x$ constant and differentiate with respect to $y$. Write $N=e^{- x y^{2}}$ and $D=x - y^{2} + 1$. The component derivatives are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nN_{y}&=- 2 x y e^{- x y^{2}}, \\\\\nD_{y}&=- 2 y.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe chain rule differentiates the exponent first; its inner derivative is $- 2 x y$. Apply the quotient rule, keeping its subtraction and squared denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}&=\\frac{N_{y}D-ND_{y}}{D^2} \\\\\n&=\\frac{\\left(- 2 x y e^{- x y^{2}}\\right)D-N\\left(- 2 y\\right)}{D^2} \\\\\n&=\\frac{e^{- x y^{2}}\\left(- 2 x^{2} y + 2 x y^{3} - 2 x y + 2 y\\right)}{\\left(x - y^{2} + 1\\right)^2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last step factors the common nonzero exponential $e^{- x y^{2}}$ from the numerator.  These expressions apply where $x - y^{2} + 1\\ne0$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186703576 :question/problem "For the function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{e^{- x y^{2}}}{x - y^{2}},\n$$\n\nfind $\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}$, where $x - y^{2}\\ne0$." :question/worked-solution "Hold $x$ constant and differentiate with respect to $y$. Write $N=e^{- x y^{2}}$ and $D=x - y^{2}$. The component derivatives are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nN_{y}&=- 2 x y e^{- x y^{2}}, \\\\\nD_{y}&=- 2 y.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe chain rule differentiates the exponent first; its inner derivative is $- 2 x y$. Apply the quotient rule, keeping its subtraction and squared denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}&=\\frac{N_{y}D-ND_{y}}{D^2} \\\\\n&=\\frac{\\left(- 2 x y e^{- x y^{2}}\\right)D-N\\left(- 2 y\\right)}{D^2} \\\\\n&=\\frac{e^{- x y^{2}}\\left(- 2 x^{2} y + 2 x y^{3} + 2 y\\right)}{\\left(x - y^{2}\\right)^2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last step factors the common nonzero exponential $e^{- x y^{2}}$ from the numerator.  These expressions apply where $x - y^{2}\\ne0$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186703580 :question/problem "For the function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{e^{- x y^{2}}}{x + y^{2} + 1},\n$$\n\nfind $\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}$, where $x + y^{2} + 1\\ne0$." :question/worked-solution "Hold $y$ constant and differentiate with respect to $x$. Write $N=e^{- x y^{2}}$ and $D=x + y^{2} + 1$. The component derivatives are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nN_{x}&=- y^{2} e^{- x y^{2}}, \\\\\nD_{x}&=1.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe chain rule differentiates the exponent first; its inner derivative is $- y^{2}$. Apply the quotient rule, keeping its subtraction and squared denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}&=\\frac{N_{x}D-ND_{x}}{D^2} \\\\\n&=\\frac{\\left(- y^{2} e^{- x y^{2}}\\right)D-N\\left(1\\right)}{D^2} \\\\\n&=\\frac{e^{- x y^{2}}\\left(- x y^{2} - y^{4} - y^{2} - 1\\right)}{\\left(x + y^{2} + 1\\right)^2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last step factors the common nonzero exponential $e^{- x y^{2}}$ from the numerator.  These expressions apply where $x + y^{2} + 1\\ne0$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186703589 :question/problem "For the function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{e^{- x y^{2}}}{x + y^{2} - 1},\n$$\n\nfind $\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}$, where $x + y^{2} - 1\\ne0$." :question/worked-solution "Hold $y$ constant and differentiate with respect to $x$. Write $N=e^{- x y^{2}}$ and $D=x + y^{2} - 1$. The component derivatives are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nN_{x}&=- y^{2} e^{- x y^{2}}, \\\\\nD_{x}&=1.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe chain rule differentiates the exponent first; its inner derivative is $- y^{2}$. Apply the quotient rule, keeping its subtraction and squared denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}&=\\frac{N_{x}D-ND_{x}}{D^2} \\\\\n&=\\frac{\\left(- y^{2} e^{- x y^{2}}\\right)D-N\\left(1\\right)}{D^2} \\\\\n&=\\frac{e^{- x y^{2}}\\left(- x y^{2} - y^{4} + y^{2} - 1\\right)}{\\left(x + y^{2} - 1\\right)^2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last step factors the common nonzero exponential $e^{- x y^{2}}$ from the numerator.  These expressions apply where $x + y^{2} - 1\\ne0$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186703594 :question/problem "For the function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{e^{- x y^{2}}}{x + y^{2} - 2},\n$$\n\nfind $\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}$, where $x + y^{2} - 2\\ne0$." :question/worked-solution "Hold $x$ constant and differentiate with respect to $y$. Write $N=e^{- x y^{2}}$ and $D=x + y^{2} - 2$. The component derivatives are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nN_{y}&=- 2 x y e^{- x y^{2}}, \\\\\nD_{y}&=2 y.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe chain rule differentiates the exponent first; its inner derivative is $- 2 x y$. Apply the quotient rule, keeping its subtraction and squared denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}&=\\frac{N_{y}D-ND_{y}}{D^2} \\\\\n&=\\frac{\\left(- 2 x y e^{- x y^{2}}\\right)D-N\\left(2 y\\right)}{D^2} \\\\\n&=\\frac{e^{- x y^{2}}\\left(- 2 x^{2} y - 2 x y^{3} + 4 x y - 2 y\\right)}{\\left(x + y^{2} - 2\\right)^2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last step factors the common nonzero exponential $e^{- x y^{2}}$ from the numerator.  These expressions apply where $x + y^{2} - 2\\ne0$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186703607 :question/problem "For $f(x,y)=\\left(x + \\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2}}\\right) \\left(x^{2} + y\\right)$, evaluate $f_x(-3,4)$. Assume $(x,y)\\ne(0,0)$." :question/worked-solution "Differentiate with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as constant. Write $f=Cuv+r$, where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nC&=1,\\quad r=0\\\\\nu&=x^{2} + y,\\quad v=x + \\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the power, sum, and chain rules on the active factors gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu_x&=2 x\\\\\nv_x&=\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2}}} + 1\\\\\nr_x&=0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApply both terms of the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_x&=C(u_xv+uv_x)+r_x\\\\\n&=2 x \\left(x + \\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2}}\\right)\\\\\n&\\quad +\\left(\\left(x^{2} + y\\right) \\left(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2}}} + 1\\right)\\right)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow substitute $x=-3$ and $y=4$ and combine the exact values:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_x(-3,4)&=\\left(-6\\right)\\left(2\\right)+\\left(13\\right)\\left(\\frac{2}{5}\\right)\\\\\n&=- \\frac{34}{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186703608 :question/problem "For $f(x,y)=\\left(x + y^{2}\\right) \\left(- y + \\ln{\\left(x y \\right)}\\right)$, evaluate $f_y(2,2)$. Assume $x y>0$." :question/worked-solution "Differentiate with respect to $y$, treating $x$ as constant. Write $f=Cuv+r$, where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nC&=1,\\quad r=0\\\\\nu&=x + y^{2},\\quad v=- y + \\ln{\\left(x y \\right)}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the power, sum, and chain rules on the active factors gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu_y&=2 y\\\\\nv_y&=-1 + \\frac{1}{y}\\\\\nr_y&=0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApply both terms of the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_y&=C(u_yv+uv_y)+r_y\\\\\n&=2 y \\left(- y + \\ln{\\left(x y \\right)}\\right)\\\\\n&\\quad +\\left(\\left(-1 + \\frac{1}{y}\\right) \\left(x + y^{2}\\right)\\right)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow substitute $x=2$ and $y=2$ and combine the exact values:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_y(2,2)&=\\left(4\\right)\\left(-2 + \\ln{\\left(4 \\right)}\\right)+\\left(6\\right)\\left(- \\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\\\\n&=-11 + 4 \\ln{\\left(4 \\right)}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186703621 :question/problem "For $f(x,y)=\\left(x + \\ln{\\left(x y \\right)}\\right) \\left(x^{2} + y\\right)$, evaluate $f_x(1,1)$. Assume $x y>0$." :question/worked-solution "Differentiate with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as constant. Write $f=Cuv+r$, where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nC&=1,\\quad r=0\\\\\nu&=x^{2} + y,\\quad v=x + \\ln{\\left(x y \\right)}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the power, sum, and chain rules on the active factors gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu_x&=2 x\\\\\nv_x&=1 + \\frac{1}{x}\\\\\nr_x&=0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApply both terms of the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_x&=C(u_xv+uv_x)+r_x\\\\\n&=2 x \\left(x + \\ln{\\left(x y \\right)}\\right)\\\\\n&\\quad +\\left(\\left(1 + \\frac{1}{x}\\right) \\left(x^{2} + y\\right)\\right)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow substitute $x=1$ and $y=1$ and combine the exact values:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_x(1,1)&=\\left(2\\right)\\left(1\\right)+\\left(2\\right)\\left(2\\right)\\\\\n&=6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186703629 :question/problem "For $f(x,y)=\\left(x^{2} + y\\right) \\left(e^{x y} + \\ln{\\left(x \\right)}\\right)$, evaluate $f_x(2,-1)$. Assume $x>0$." :question/worked-solution "Differentiate with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as constant. Write $f=Cuv+r$, where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nC&=1,\\quad r=0\\\\\nu&=x^{2} + y,\\quad v=e^{x y} + \\ln{\\left(x \\right)}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the power, sum, and chain rules on the active factors gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu_x&=2 x\\\\\nv_x&=y e^{x y} + \\frac{1}{x}\\\\\nr_x&=0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApply both terms of the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_x&=C(u_xv+uv_x)+r_x\\\\\n&=2 x \\left(e^{x y} + \\ln{\\left(x \\right)}\\right)\\\\\n&\\quad +\\left(\\left(x^{2} + y\\right) \\left(y e^{x y} + \\frac{1}{x}\\right)\\right)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow substitute $x=2$ and $y=-1$ and combine the exact values:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_x(2,-1)&=\\left(4\\right)\\left(e^{-2} + \\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}\\right)+\\left(3\\right)\\left(\\frac{1}{2} - e^{-2}\\right)\\\\\n&=e^{-2} + \\frac{3}{2} + 4 \\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186703671 :question/problem "For the function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=x y^{2} + \\ln{\\left(e^{x - y^{2}} + 1 \\right)},\n$$\n\nevaluate both partial derivatives at $(1,2)$.\n\n$f_x(1,2)=$\n\n{{field-1}}\n\n$f_y(1,2)=$\n\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "Treat $y$ as constant and differentiate with respect to $x$. Apply the chain rule, and the sum and constant multiple rules where needed.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu&=x - y^{2}\\\\\n\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial x}&=1\\\\\n\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\ln(1+e^u)&=\\frac{1}{1+e^u}\\cdot e^u\\cdot\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial x}\\\\\n\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\left(x y^{2}\\right)&=y^{2}\\\\\nf_x(x,y)&=\\left(\\frac{e^{u}}{e^{u} + 1}\\right)\\cdot\\left(1\\right)+\\left(y^{2}\\right)\\\\\n&=y^{2} + \\frac{e^{x - y^{2}}}{e^{x - y^{2}} + 1}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute $x=1$ and $y=2$ after differentiating. The inner input is\n\n$$\nu_0=\\left(1\\right) - \\left(2\\right)^{2}=-3.\n$$\n\nUsing this value in the chain factors gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_x(1,2)&=\\left(\\frac{e^{u_0}}{e^{u_0} + 1}\\right)\\cdot\\left(1\\right)+\\left(4\\right)\\\\\n&=\\left(\\frac{1}{\\left(e^{-3} + 1\\right) e^{3}}\\right)\\cdot\\left(1\\right)+\\left(4\\right)\\\\\n&=\\frac{5 + 4 e^{3}}{1 + e^{3}}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTreat $x$ as constant and differentiate with respect to $y$. Apply the chain rule, and the sum and constant multiple rules where needed.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu&=x - y^{2}\\\\\n\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial y}&=- 2 y\\\\\n\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}\\ln(1+e^u)&=\\frac{1}{1+e^u}\\cdot e^u\\cdot\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial y}\\\\\n\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}\\left(x y^{2}\\right)&=2 x y\\\\\nf_y(x,y)&=\\left(\\frac{e^{u}}{e^{u} + 1}\\right)\\cdot\\left(- 2 y\\right)+\\left(2 x y\\right)\\\\\n&=2 x y - \\frac{2 y e^{x - y^{2}}}{e^{x - y^{2}} + 1}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute $x=1$ and $y=2$ after differentiating. The inner input is\n\n$$\nu_0=\\left(1\\right) - \\left(2\\right)^{2}=-3.\n$$\n\nUsing this value in the chain factors gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_y(1,2)&=\\left(\\frac{e^{u_0}}{e^{u_0} + 1}\\right)\\cdot\\left(-4\\right)+\\left(4\\right)\\\\\n&=\\left(\\frac{1}{\\left(e^{-3} + 1\\right) e^{3}}\\right)\\cdot\\left(-4\\right)+\\left(4\\right)\\\\\n&=\\frac{4 e^{3}}{1 + e^{3}}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThese derivatives apply for all real $x,y$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186703689 :question/problem "For the function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=- x^{2} y + \\ln{\\left(e^{x^{2} + y} + 1 \\right)},\n$$\n\nevaluate both partial derivatives at $(-1,-2)$.\n\n$f_x(-1,-2)=$\n\n{{field-1}}\n\n$f_y(-1,-2)=$\n\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "Treat $y$ as constant and differentiate with respect to $x$. Apply the chain rule, and the sum and constant multiple rules where needed.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu&=x^{2} + y\\\\\n\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial x}&=2 x\\\\\n\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\ln(1+e^u)&=\\frac{1}{1+e^u}\\cdot e^u\\cdot\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial x}\\\\\n\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\left(- x^{2} y\\right)&=- 2 x y\\\\\nf_x(x,y)&=\\left(\\frac{e^{u}}{e^{u} + 1}\\right)\\cdot\\left(2 x\\right)+\\left(- 2 x y\\right)\\\\\n&=- 2 x y + \\frac{2 x e^{x^{2} + y}}{e^{x^{2} + y} + 1}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute $x=-1$ and $y=-2$ after differentiating. The inner input is\n\n$$\nu_0=\\left(-1\\right)^{2} + \\left(-2\\right)=-1.\n$$\n\nUsing this value in the chain factors gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_x(-1,-2)&=\\left(\\frac{e^{u_0}}{e^{u_0} + 1}\\right)\\cdot\\left(-2\\right)+\\left(-4\\right)\\\\\n&=\\left(\\frac{1}{e \\left(e^{-1} + 1\\right)}\\right)\\cdot\\left(-2\\right)+\\left(-4\\right)\\\\\n&=\\frac{2 \\left(- 2 e - 3\\right)}{1 + e}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTreat $x$ as constant and differentiate with respect to $y$. Apply the chain rule, and the sum and constant multiple rules where needed.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu&=x^{2} + y\\\\\n\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial y}&=1\\\\\n\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}\\ln(1+e^u)&=\\frac{1}{1+e^u}\\cdot e^u\\cdot\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial y}\\\\\n\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}\\left(- x^{2} y\\right)&=- x^{2}\\\\\nf_y(x,y)&=\\left(\\frac{e^{u}}{e^{u} + 1}\\right)\\cdot\\left(1\\right)+\\left(- x^{2}\\right)\\\\\n&=- x^{2} + \\frac{e^{x^{2} + y}}{e^{x^{2} + y} + 1}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute $x=-1$ and $y=-2$ after differentiating. The inner input is\n\n$$\nu_0=\\left(-1\\right)^{2} + \\left(-2\\right)=-1.\n$$\n\nUsing this value in the chain factors gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_y(-1,-2)&=\\left(\\frac{e^{u_0}}{e^{u_0} + 1}\\right)\\cdot\\left(1\\right)+\\left(-1\\right)\\\\\n&=\\left(\\frac{1}{e \\left(e^{-1} + 1\\right)}\\right)\\cdot\\left(1\\right)+\\left(-1\\right)\\\\\n&=- \\frac{e}{1 + e}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThese derivatives apply for all real $x,y$."}]}
